## 4. Warehouse Graph Engine & Pathfinding Algorithms

### 4.1 Graph Abstraction Design
The indoor dark-store floor is abstracted into an adjacency-list graph $G = (V, E, W)$:
- **Vertices ($V$)**: Structural decision points, aisle intersections, zone boundaries, and storage location access points (`warehouse_nodes`).
- **Edges ($E$)**: Walkable corridors connecting adjacent nodes (`warehouse_edges`).
- **Weights ($W$)**: Physical Euclidean or Manhattan distance in meters (`distance_meters`).

```python
# Graph Engine Domain Classes (Conceptual)
class WarehouseNode:
    id: int
    node_code: str
    zone_id: int
    x_coord: float
    y_coord: float
    is_blocked: bool = False

class WarehouseEdge:
    from_node_id: int
    to_node_id: int
    distance_meters: float
    is_traversable: bool = True

class WarehouseGraph:
    nodes: Dict[int, WarehouseNode]
    adjacency: Dict[int, List[Tuple[int, float]]]  # node_id -> [(neighbor_id, weight)]
    
    def load_from_db(self, warehouse_id: int, db: Session) -> None: ...
    def get_shortest_path(self, start_id: int, goal_id: int, algorithm: str = "a_star") -> PathResult: ...
```

### 4.2 Pathfinding Algorithm Evaluation & Comparison

| Parameter | Dijkstra's Algorithm | A* (A-Star) Algorithm |
|---|---|---|
| **Mechanism** | Uniform-cost search exploring nodes in order of path cost $g(n)$ | Heuristic-guided search exploring nodes by $f(n) = g(n) + h(n)$ |
| **Heuristic $h(n)$** | None ($h(n) = 0$) | Euclidean Distance $h(n) = \sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2}$ |
| **Time Complexity** | $O((\|E\| + \|V\|) \log \|V\|)$ using Fibonacci/Binary Min-Heap | $O((\|E\| + \|V\|) \log \|V\|)$ worst-case; $O(\|E\|)$ with tight heuristic |
| **Memory Complexity** | $O(\|V\|)$ to store open/closed node states | $O(\|V\|)$ to store priority queue and distance maps |
| **Optimality** | Guaranteed optimal for all non-negative edge weights | Guaranteed optimal if $h(n)$ is admissible ($h(n) \le h^*(n)$) |
| **Primary Use Case** | All-pairs shortest path matrix precomputation (Floyd-Warshall / Multi-source Dijkstra) | Single-source to single-destination point-to-point routing |

> [!NOTE]
> **Graph Policy**: Use **Dijkstra** for precomputing the $N \times N$ distance matrix between all storage locations during batch setup, and **A*** for dynamic point-to-point route evaluation when real-time obstacles or blocked edges are present.

### 4.3 Pathfinding Execution Flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Request: Start Node A, End Node B] --> B{Node A & B Valid?}
    B -- No --> C[Return Error: Invalid Node ID]
    B -- Yes --> D{Are Nodes in Same Graph Component?}
    D -- No/Unreachable --> E[Return Status: UNREACHABLE, Distance: Infinity]
    D -- Yes --> F[Initialize Priority Queue Q with Start Node]
    F --> G[Extract Node with Min f_score]
    G --> H{Is Target Node B?}
    H -- Yes --> I[Reconstruct Path via CameFrom Map]
    H -- No --> J[Expand Neighbors & Evaluate Edge Traversability]
    J --> K[Compute Tentative g_score & Heuristic h_score]
    K --> G
    I --> L[Return PathResult: Nodes, Distance Meters, Latency MS]
```

